## Description

A user wants to access the website `www.foobar.com`.  
The domain name `foobar.com` has a **DNS A record** pointing `www` → `8.8.8.8`.  
The user's browser resolves the domain via DNS, connects to server IP **8.8.8.8**, sends an HTTP/HTTPS request to the web server (Nginx).  
Nginx serves static files or forwards dynamic requests to the application server, which processes the application code and communicates with the MySQL database if data is needed.  
The response travels back to the user via the Internet using HTTP/TCP.

---

## Components Used

| Component | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| Domain Name: `foobar.com` | Human-friendly access instead of using IP |
| DNS `www` Record | A record pointing `www.foobar.com -> 8.8.8.8` |
| Server | Physical/virtual machine hosting all components |
| Web Server (Nginx) | Receives HTTP requests, serves static content, forwards dynamic requests |
| Application Server | Runs backend logic, executes code, talks to DB |
| Application Files | Code base for website functionality |
| Database (MySQL) | Stores persistent data accessed by application |

---

## Communication

The server communicates with the user's computer over the **Internet using HTTP/HTTPS over TCP/IP**.

---

## Issues / Weaknesses

1. **SPOF – Single Point of Failure**  
   Entire infrastructure depends on one machine. If server fails, website goes down.

2. **Maintenance causes downtime**  
   Updating code, restarting Nginx or DB will stop the service temporarily.

3. **No scalability**  
   One server cannot handle high traffic; performance degrades under heavy load.

